<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Margaret of Anjou</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1430">1430</a>–<a href="/years/1482">1482</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/15th-century">15th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Margaret of Anjou was queen of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a> as the wife of Henry VI from 1445. As her husband's mental illness left him unable to rule, she became the driving force of the Lancastrian cause in the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/wars-of-the-roses">Wars of the Roses</a>, raising armies against the House of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a>. Defeated at Tewkesbury in 1471, where her only son was killed, she was imprisoned and later ransomed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI of France</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/margaret-of-anjou.jpg" alt="Margaret of Anjou" width="320" height="363" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Talbot Master (fl. in Rouen, c. 1430–60) · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:MargaretAnjou.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Early life and background</h2><p>Margaret was born on 23 March 1430 at Pont-à-Mousson in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lorraine">Lorraine</a>, the second daughter of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/rene-of-anjou">René of Anjou</a>, titular king of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/naples">Naples</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sicily">Sicily</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/jerusalem">Jerusalem</a>, and Isabella, Duchess of Lorraine. Several women of her family exercised power as regents, among them her mother and her grandmother Yolande of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-aragon">Aragon</a>, which may have shaped her later actions.</p><p>In 1444 she was betrothed by proxy to Henry VI of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/england">England</a>, in a marriage negotiated principally by William de la Pole, Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/suffolk">Suffolk</a>, as part of peace efforts near the end of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hundred-years-war">Hundred Years' War</a>. She married Henry at Titchfield Abbey on 23 April 1445 and was crowned at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/westminster-abbey">Westminster Abbey</a> on 30 May. The loss of Maine to Anjou, negotiated soon afterwards, was deeply unpopular.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Career</h2><p><a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/prince-henry-the-navigator">Henry</a>'s mental condition was unstable when they married, and by the birth of their only son, Edward of Westminster, on 13 October 1453, he had suffered a complete breakdown. Richard of York, 3rd Duke of York, was <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/lord-protector">Lord Protector</a> in 1453 and 1454. In May 1455 Margaret and Henry called a Great Council that excluded the Yorkists, and York marched south to meet the Lancastrian army, which was crushed at the First Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-albans">St Albans</a> on 22 May. Historians differ on when her enmity with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> began. Helen Maurer places it in 1455, while Paul Murray Kendall held that it came earlier.</p><p>Margaret's commander Henry Beaufort, 3rd Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/somerset">Somerset</a>, won the Battle of Wakefield on 30 December 1460, where <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/york">York</a> was killed. She won the Second Battle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/st-albans">St Albans</a> on 17 February 1461. The Lancastrians lost at Towton on 29 March 1461, and Edward, York's son, deposed Henry and took the throne as Edward IV. Margaret fled to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> and then <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a>, where, at the instigation of her cousin <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a>, she accepted an alliance with <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/warwick">Warwick</a>, sealed by the marriage of her son to Warwick's daughter Anne Neville.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Warwick was defeated and killed at the Battle of Barnet on 14 April 1471. At the Battle of Tewkesbury on 4 May, Margaret led her own army to defeat, and her seventeen-year-old son was killed. It is not known whether he died in the fighting or was executed afterwards by the Duke of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glarentza">Clarence</a>. She was taken captive and in 1475 was ransomed by <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/louis-xi">Louis XI</a>. She lived in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/france">France</a> for seven years as a poor relation of the king, and died on 25 August 1482 at the castle of Dampierre-sur-<a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/loire">Loire</a>, aged 52.</p><p>She obtained a licence on 30 March 1448 to found Queens' College, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/cambridge">Cambridge</a>, and many of her letters survive. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/william-shakespeare">William Shakespeare</a> made her a major character in his first group of history plays.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Margaret of Anjou” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1461, during the Wars of the Roses, Mont Orgueil was handed to the French under an agreement made by <b>Margaret of Anjou</b> with Pierre de Brézé, and the island remained under French control until Sir Richard Harliston recovered it in 1468.</blockquote><a href="/empire/bailiwick-of-jersey">Bailiwick of Jersey · Military</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-pont-a-mousson">Pont-à-Mousson</a>, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. Died in Souzay-Champigny, in what is now <a href="/countries/france">France</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=48.9,6.05&amp;year=1463" aria-label="Pont-à-Mousson on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4690 456 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Margaret of Anjou"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M5140 726h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M5140 726h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=48.9,6.05&amp;year=1463">What was here in 1463? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
